WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.580
What can an organ send through the body without any nerves?

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.120
And what decides how much of it is in the blood?

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.460
A physician in Göttingen asked the first question with young cockerels.

4
00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:17.813
He castrated them.

5
00:00:19.013 --> 00:00:22.673
Without testes, the birds grew small combs, and they crowed little.

6
00:00:23.973 --> 00:00:26.773
In some birds he put a testis back into the belly.

7
00:00:28.013 --> 00:00:29.953
It was cut off from its original nerves.

8
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:33.353
Those birds still grew combs and crowed.

9
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:36.893
So the graft must have sent something through the blood.

10
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:39.433
That something is a hormone.

11
00:00:40.693 --> 00:00:44.673
A hormone is a chemical released by one group of cells into the blood.

12
00:00:45.813 --> 00:00:47.753
The blood carries it to distant cells.

13
00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:51.253
Only cells with a receptor for it respond.

14
00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.267
Now picture the blood as a tank.

15
00:00:55.447 --> 00:00:57.607
The gland is a tap, pouring hormone in.

16
00:00:58.827 --> 00:01:02.907
A drain stands for every way the body removes or breaks down the hormone.

17
00:01:04.107 --> 00:01:06.927
The water line is the hormone's level in the blood.

18
00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.967
It settles where what drains out matches what pours in.

19
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.140
In tank terms, castration shut a tap, and the male traits faded.

20
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.280
The graft opened a tap again, and the traits came back.

21
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.740
Berthold never measured the hormone itself.

22
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.380
In mammals, one tap can drive another.

23
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.080
Take the human stress axis.

24
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.580
The hypothalamus releases CRH.

25
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.520
Short veins called portal vessels carry it straight to the anterior pituitary.

26
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.060
The pituitary then releases ACTH.

27
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.040
And ACTH makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol.

28
00:01:49.283 --> 00:01:55.643
Now add a sensor. Cortisol acts back on the hypothalamus and the pituitary, turning

29
00:01:55.643 --> 00:02:00.543
their taps down. When cortisol rises, the drive to make it falls.

30
00:02:01.843 --> 00:02:03.523
When it drops, the drive rises.

31
00:02:04.463 --> 00:02:09.503
This is negative feedback, a response that opposes the change that caused it.

32
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:12.883
Suppose the adrenal glands are removed.

33
00:02:14.343 --> 00:02:16.783
Cortisol falls, because nothing secretes it.

34
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:19.123
What happens to ACTH?

35
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:31.630
ACTH rises. With no cortisol feeding back, a key brake is gone.

36
00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.390
The hypothalamus and pituitary drive harder, but no adrenal can answer.

37
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.790
Taking a gland away shows how the rest of the loop responds.

38
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:45.713
Feedback does not freeze the level at one value.

39
00:02:46.993 --> 00:02:50.093
In humans, cortisol rises and falls over the day.

40
00:02:51.313 --> 00:02:55.413
It also comes in pulses, roughly every sixty to ninety minutes.

41
00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:57.913
Feedback adjusts the tap.

42
00:02:59.073 --> 00:03:01.633
It shapes a pattern rather than fixing a number.

43
00:03:02.767 --> 00:03:07.147
So a hormone's level is a balance of secretion in and clearance out.

44
00:03:08.527 --> 00:03:09.867
Feedback works on the secretion.

45
00:03:11.227 --> 00:03:14.587
How fast the level can follow is a question about the drain.
